### Important Hardware Conflict Regarding Pins PA2 and PA3

On the **STM32G474**, pins **PA2 and PA3 do not connect to USART1** in hardware:
- **PA2** alternate functions include **USART2_TX** (AF7) and **LPUART1_TX** (AF12).
- **PA3** alternate functions include **USART2_RX** (AF7) and **LPUART1_RX** (AF12).
- **USART1** TX and RX pins on STM32G474 are:
  - TX: **PA9** (AF7), **PB6** (AF7), **PC4** (AF7), **PE0** (AF7)
  - RX: **PA10** (AF7), **PB7** (AF7), **PC5** (AF7), **PE1** (AF7)

Because you noted that USART2 is used for RS-485 and you specifically need the console on **USART1**, the standard pinout for USART1 on Port A is **PA9 (TX)** and **PA10 (RX)**. 

Below are two implementations:
1. **Primary Solution:** **USART1** on its native Port A pins (**PA9 = TX, PA10 = RX**).
2. **Alternative Solution:** If your schematic actually routed the console connector to **PA2 and PA3**, those physical pins only map to **USART2** (or LPUART1), so the corresponding USART2 initialization is also provided.

---

### Clock and Baud-Rate Calculation

- $f_{\text{PCLK2}} = 170\text{ MHz}$ (with AHB prescaler = 1, APB2 prescaler = 1, and `RCC->CCIPR.USART1SEL = 0b00` [PCLK default]).
- Oversampling by 16 (`OVER8 = 0`).
- Baud rate $= 115200\text{ bps}$.
$$\text{USARTDIV} = \frac{f_{\text{ck}}}{\text{Baud}} = \frac{170\,000\,000}{115\,200} \approx 1475.6944$$
Rounding to the nearest integer for `USART_BRR`:
$$\text{BRR} = 1476 = \text{0x05C4}$$
$$\text{Actual Baud Rate} = \frac{170\,000\,000}{1476} \approx 115176.15\text{ bps (error } \approx -0.02\%\text{)}$$

---

### Solution 1: USART1 on PA9 (TX) and PA10 (RX) (Recommended)

```c
#include "stm32g4xx.h"

void usart1_console_init(void)
{
    /* 1. Enable peripheral clocks for GPIOA and USART1 */
    RCC->AHB2ENR |= RCC_AHB2ENR_GPIOAEN;
    RCC->APB2ENR |= RCC_APB2ENR_USART1EN;

    /* Optional delay/dummy read to allow peripheral clock to stabilize */
    __NOP();
    __NOP();

    /* 2. Configure PA9 (TX) and PA10 (RX) for Alternate Function 7 (AF7) */
    /* Set MODER to Alternate Function mode (0b10) for PA9 and PA10 */
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(GPIO_MODER_MODE9_Msk | GPIO_MODER_MODE10_Msk);
    GPIOA->MODER |=  (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE9_Pos) | (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE10_Pos);

    /* Output push-pull on PA9 (OTYPER bit 9 = 0) */
    GPIOA->OTYPER &= ~GPIO_OTYPER_OT9;

    /* Very high speed on PA9 and PA10 (0b11) */
    GPIOA->OSPEEDR |= (3U << GPIO_OSPEEDR_OSPEED9_Pos) | (3U << GPIO_OSPEEDR_OSPEED10_Pos);

    /* Pull-up on PA10 (RX) to prevent floating line when disconnected */
    GPIOA->PUPDR &= ~(GPIO_PUPDR_PUPD9_Msk | GPIO_PUPDR_PUPD10_Msk);
    GPIOA->PUPDR |=  (1U << GPIO_PUPDR_PUPD10_Pos);

    /* Connect PA9 and PA10 to AF7 (USART1) via AFRH */
    GPIOA->AFR[1] &= ~((0xFU << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL9_Pos) | (0xFU << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL10_Pos));
    GPIOA->AFR[1] |=  (7U << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL9_Pos) | (7U << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL10_Pos);

    /* 3. Configure USART1:
     * - Disable USART (UE = 0)
     * - 8 data bits, no parity: M1:M0 = 00, PCE = 0
     * - Oversampling by 16: OVER8 = 0
     * - 1 stop bit: STOP[1:0] = 00 in CR2
     */
    USART1->CR1 = 0;
    USART1->CR2 = 0;
    USART1->CR3 = 0;

    /* 4. Write Baud Rate Register before enabling the peripheral:
     *    BRR = 170,000,000 / 115200 = 1476 = 0x05C4
     */
    USART1->BRR = 1476U;

    /* 5. Enable Transmitter, Receiver, and USART peripheral */
    USART1->CR1 |= USART_CR1_TE | USART_CR1_RE | USART_CR1_UE;

    /* 6. Wait for TE and RE acknowledge flags */
    while ((USART1->ISR & (USART_ISR_TEACK | USART_ISR_REACK)) != (USART_ISR_TEACK | USART_ISR_REACK))
    {
        /* Busy-wait until transmitter and receiver are active */
    }
}
```

---

### Solution 2: If Hardware Actually Routes PA2 / PA3 (USART2)

If your PCB tracks physically link the console port to **PA2 and PA3**, you must configure **USART2** (clocked on APB1 at 170 MHz, same BRR = 1476):

```c
#include "stm32g4xx.h"

void usart2_console_init_pa2_pa3(void)
{
    /* 1. Enable peripheral clocks for GPIOA and USART2 */
    RCC->AHB2ENR  |= RCC_AHB2ENR_GPIOAEN;
    RCC->APB1ENR1 |= RCC_APB1ENR1_USART2EN;

    __NOP();
    __NOP();

    /* 2. Configure PA2 (TX) and PA3 (RX) for Alternate Function AF7 (USART2) */
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(GPIO_MODER_MODE2_Msk | GPIO_MODER_MODE3_Msk);
    GPIOA->MODER |=  (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE2_Pos) | (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE3_Pos);

    /* Push-pull on PA2 */
    GPIOA->OTYPER &= ~GPIO_OTYPER_OT2;

    /* Very high speed */
    GPIOA->OSPEEDR |= (3U << GPIO_OSPEEDR_OSPEED2_Pos) | (3U << GPIO_OSPEEDR_OSPEED3_Pos);

    /* Pull-up on PA3 (RX) */
    GPIOA->PUPDR &= ~(GPIO_PUPDR_PUPD2_Msk | GPIO_PUPDR_PUPD3_Msk);
    GPIOA->PUPDR |=  (1U << GPIO_PUPDR_PUPD3_Pos);

    /* Connect PA2 and PA3 to AF7 via AFRL */
    GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~((0xFU << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL2_Pos) | (0xFU << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL3_Pos));
    GPIOA->AFR[0] |=  (7U << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL2_Pos) | (7U << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL3_Pos);

    /* 3. Configure USART2 (8N1, OVER8=0) */
    USART2->CR1 = 0;
    USART2->CR2 = 0;
    USART2->CR3 = 0;

    /* 4. Set BRR before UE (PCLK1 = 170 MHz) */
    USART2->BRR = 1476U;

    /* 5. Enable TE, RE, and UE */
    USART2->CR1 |= USART_CR1_TE | USART_CR1_RE | USART_CR1_UE;

    while ((USART2->ISR & (USART_ISR_TEACK | USART_ISR_REACK)) != (USART_ISR_TEACK | USART_ISR_REACK))
    {
    }
}
```

