### Bare-Metal C Code

```c
#include "stm32g4xx.h"

void tim3_ch1_pwm_init(void)
{
    /* 1. Enable GPIOA peripheral clock */
    RCC->AHB2ENR |= RCC_AHB2ENR_GPIOAEN;

    /* 2. Configure PA6 as Alternate Function mode (MODER6 = 10b) */
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE6_Msk;
    GPIOA->MODER |= (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE6_Pos);

    /* 3. Select AF2 (TIM3_CH1) for PA6 in GPIOA_AFRL */
    GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~(0xFU << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL6_Pos);
    GPIOA->AFR[0] |= (2U << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL6_Pos);

    /* 4. Enable TIM3 peripheral clock */
    RCC->APB1ENR1 |= RCC_APB1ENR1_TIM3EN;

    /* 5. Set timer prescaler and auto-reload values:
     *    Timer clock = 170 MHz
     *    PWM frequency = 170 MHz / ((0 + 1) * (8499 + 1)) = 20 kHz
     */
    TIM3->PSC = 0;
    TIM3->ARR = 8499;

    /* 6. Set compare value for 40 % duty cycle:
     *    CCR1 = 8500 * 40% = 3400
     */
    TIM3->CCR1 = 3400;

    /* 7. Configure TIM3 Channel 1:
     *    - CC1S = 00 (Channel 1 configured as output)
     *    - OC1M = 0110b (PWM mode 1)
     *    - OC1PE = 1 (Output compare 1 preload enable)
     */
    TIM3->CCMR1 &= ~(TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Msk | TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Msk);
    TIM3->CCMR1 |= (6U << TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Pos) | TIM_CCMR1_OC1PE;

    /* 8. Enable Channel 1 output with active-high polarity (CC1P = 0 default) */
    TIM3->CCER |= TIM_CCER_CC1E;

    /* 9. Enable Auto-reload preload (ARPE) and enable counter (CEN) */
    TIM3->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_ARPE | TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
```

### Hardware Constraint Analysis: Shared Timer Counter

In the STM32G474 architecture, **all channels of a general-purpose timer such as TIM3 share the same counter (`CNT`), prescaler (`PSC`), and auto-reload register (`ARR`)** [STM32G474_RM0440 p.1326].

The output PWM frequency is determined strictly by the timer time base:
$$f_{\text{PWM}} = \frac{f_{\text{TIM\_CLK}}}{(\text{PSC} + 1) \times (\text{ARR} + 1)}$$

Because TIM3 is configured for **20 kHz** on Channel 1 (PA6) with:
- $f_{\text{TIM\_CLK}} = 170\text{ MHz}$
- $\text{PSC} = 0$
- $\text{ARR} = 8499$

it is **physically impossible for TIM3 Channel 2 (PA7) to generate a different frequency (1 kHz) at the same time**. While each channel has its own capture/compare register (`CCR1`, `CCR2`) controlling independent duty cycles, all channels of TIM3 must share the same frequency.

---

### Solutions & Alternatives

1. **Keep Both on TIM3 (Shared Frequency)**:
   If PA7 must remain connected to `TIM3_CH2` (AF2), both PA6 and PA7 will share the same timer frequency. Below, the implementation configures Channel 1 (PA6) at 40% duty cycle and Channel 2 (PA7) at 50% duty cycle.
2. **Use an Independent Timer on PA7 (`TIM17_CH1`, AF1)**:
   If 1 kHz is required for the buzzer, pin PA7 can instead be mapped to **TIM17_CH1** (Alternate Function 1) [STM32G474_Datasheet p.73]. TIM17 has its own dedicated counter and prescaler, permitting an independent 1 kHz output without affecting TIM3 on PA6.
3. **Move PA6 to `TIM16_CH1` (AF1)**:
   This leaves TIM3 free to run at 1 kHz for the buzzer on PA7.

---

### Bare-Metal C Code: TIM3 Driving PA6 (CH1) and PA7 (CH2)

The following complete bare-metal C function initializes both PA6 (`TIM3_CH1`, 40% duty) and PA7 (`TIM3_CH2`, 50% duty) on `TIM3`, in edge-aligned PWM mode 1 with active-high polarity:

```c
#include "stm32g4xx.h"

void tim3_pwm_init(void)
{
    /* 1. Enable GPIOA peripheral clock */
    RCC->AHB2ENR |= RCC_AHB2ENR_GPIOAEN;

    /* 2. Configure PA6 and PA7 as Alternate Function mode (MODER = 10b) */
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(GPIO_MODER_MODE6_Msk | GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Msk);
    GPIOA->MODER |= (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE6_Pos) | (2U << GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Pos);

    /* 3. Route PA6 to AF2 (TIM3_CH1) and PA7 to AF2 (TIM3_CH2) in GPIOA_AFRL */
    GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~((0xFU << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL6_Pos) | (0xFU << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Pos));
    GPIOA->AFR[0] |= (2U << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL6_Pos) | (2U << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Pos);

    /* 4. Enable TIM3 peripheral clock on APB1 */
    RCC->APB1ENR1 |= RCC_APB1ENR1_TIM3EN;

    /* 5. Set prescaler and auto-reload for 20 kHz PWM:
     *    Timer clock = 170 MHz
     *    f_PWM = 170 MHz / ((0 + 1) * (8499 + 1)) = 20 kHz
     */
    TIM3->PSC = 0;
    TIM3->ARR = 8499;

    /* 6. Configure duty cycles:
     *    Channel 1 (PA6, LED): 40% duty -> CCR1 = 8500 * 0.40 = 3400
     *    Channel 2 (PA7, Buzzer): 50% duty -> CCR2 = 8500 * 0.50 = 4250
     */
    TIM3->CCR1 = 3400;
    TIM3->CCR2 = 4250;

    /* 7. Configure Channels 1 and 2 in PWM mode 1 with preload enabled */
    TIM3->CCMR1 &= ~(TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Msk | TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Msk |
                     TIM_CCMR1_OC2M_Msk | TIM_CCMR1_CC2S_Msk);
    TIM3->CCMR1 |= (6U << TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Pos) | TIM_CCMR1_OC1PE |
                   (6U << TIM_CCMR1_OC2M_Pos) | TIM_CCMR1_OC2PE;

    /* 8. Enable Channel 1 and Channel 2 outputs (active-high polarity) */
    TIM3->CCER |= TIM_CCER_CC1E | TIM_CCER_CC2E;

    /* 9. Enable auto-reload preload and start timer counter */
    TIM3->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_ARPE | TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
